Content:
@4:05... I'm just taking a stab at why spin matters..

Centrifugal force, ball orientation, the texture of the ball lends to surface air interaction similar to a golf ball although golf balls have dimples.. 

On a similar note, warheads spin as they reenter the atmosphere..

"Maybe Brady is a bit more scientific than he gives himself credit for."  That makes me laugh!  I might rather say science is about trying to figure out what he already knows, but in a different way for people who can't or don't understand (or don't want to put in the time to learn by doing) what he knows.
(-;

Neat vid!

Great vid,
@veritasium - I wish you dove into the turnover a bit more.  It's possible to throw a football (even I can do this on demand) such that the ball keeps its orientation, even starting to rotate backwards in some cases, instead of pitching forward.  Your video leads the viewer to believe that pitchover is inevitable (9:18), but if you listen closer to your words "if the ball is perfectly aligned with direction of airflow..." it's clear that this is  where talent is needed (and for most athletes this is intuitive... so much physics the human brain can absorb and act on subconsciously! another avenue for deeper investigation).

When talking about kicking, you glossed over the topic very quicky and you may be correct about wanting a knuckle-ball in some cases, but the punter can also impart spin on the ball (with or without turn-over) to precisely place the ball just outside the goal line and inside the sideline.  Further, when kicking the fieldgoal, the ball is purposely kicked end-over-end to help keep it on a predictable path... would love to see the physics on that!
